## 1. Prioritized findings

**Release recommendation:** do not ship this version for the festival on-sale. Fix the blockers, then test the complete checkout—not just this step.

| Issue | Who it affects | Severity | WCAG 2.2 criterion, where applicable | Fix |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Clickable `div` is not a keyboard-operable submit button; focus outlines are removed | Keyboard, switch-control and screen-reader users | **Blocker** | 2.1.1 Keyboard; 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value; 2.4.7 Focus Visible | Use native buttons, form submission and clearly visible focus. |
| Two-minute timeout silently releases tickets and redirects, losing context | Everyone; especially people with motor, cognitive or visual disabilities | **Blocker** | 2.2.1 Timing Adjustable, unless a genuine exception applies | Warn early, provide a simple extension mechanism, and do not redirect on expiry. Preserve entered details in memory. Inventory scarcity alone does not establish an exception. |
| Image CAPTCHA has no alternative or explanation | Blind, low-vision and cognitively disabled users | **Blocker** | 1.1.1 Non-text Content | Prefer non-interactive abuse prevention. If retaining a CAPTCHA, provide equivalent accessible alternatives—not merely alt text containing the answer. |
| Placeholders disappear; fields lack persistent labels and useful instructions | Everyone, particularly screen-reader and cognitive-accessibility users | **Major** | 1.3.1 Info and Relationships; 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions | Associate visible labels, hints and errors with every field. Use logical headings and groups. |
| Email has only a red border: no explanation or recovery guidance | Screen-reader, colour-blind and other users | **Major** | 1.4.1 Use of Color; 3.3.1 Error Identification; 3.3.3 Error Suggestion | Validate actual input; show specific inline errors, `aria-invalid` and a focused, linked error summary. |
| Email typos cause failed delivery | All customers and support staff | **Major** | 3.3.4 Error Prevention (Legal, Financial, Data), for reviewing the transaction | Show the destination email prominently before payment, with an edit route. Syntax validation cannot detect every wrong address; do not silently “correct” it. |
| Hint text and white-on-orange button text have roughly 2.6:1 contrast on white/orange; input borders are faint | Low-vision users and mobile users in bright light | **Major** | 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum); 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast | Retain brand orange with dark button text; darken hints and control boundaries. |
| Missing input purposes and inappropriate types; card numbers limited to 16 characters | Mobile and autofill users; holders of longer card numbers | **Major** | 1.3.5 Identify Input Purpose; other aspects are usability defects | Add autocomplete tokens, email/tel types and numeric input modes. Permit pasted/formatted card numbers; let the payment provider determine supported cards. |
| Immediate payment lacks a visible review/correction stage in this excerpt | Everyone making a financial commitment | **Major** | 3.3.4 Error Prevention (Legal, Financial, Data) | Show tickets, final price, delivery address and masked payment information before explicit confirmation. |
| Fixed timer, cramped layout and tiny legal links create mobile/zoom risks | The 68% on mobile; magnification and touch users | **Major** | Verify 1.4.10 Reflow, 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum), 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum) | Put the timer in normal flow; use responsive layout and generous targets. Small text alone is not a WCAG failure; inline links also have target-size exceptions. |
| Newsletter is preselected and bundled into checkout | Customers who do not want marketing | **Major** | Not inherently a WCAG failure | Make marketing optional, unchecked and separate from transactional ticket delivery. |

## 2. Reply to Richard

> Richard, Lighthouse 100 is a useful automated-test result, not proof of WCAG AA conformance. It cannot establish usable timing, correct screen-reader interaction, understandable errors or an accessible end-to-end purchase. Some problems here should also be detectable automatically, so let’s confirm which build and state were tested.
>
> We will ship the newsletter **unchecked**. For EU consent-based marketing, pre-ticked boxes do not establish valid affirmative consent; national electronic-marketing rules and any existing-customer exception need legal review. US email rules are often opt-out rather than universally opt-in, but that does not justify bundling marketing into purchase. We should measure informed subscriptions and downstream engagement—not just checkbox counts.

## 3. Corrected single-file prototype

**Assumptions:** the business permits unlimited two-minute extensions; the example order is two €60 tickets with no additional fees. Production must supply the real inventory, price and currency.

This is a runnable **interaction prototype**, not a payment implementation. Production requires server-authoritative holds and atomic availability checks, PSP-hosted/tokenized payment fields, authoritative validation, consent records and accessible server-error recovery. No card data is persisted or sent here.

To preserve every supplied field, `captcha` remains as an explicitly labelled, copyable **demo verification code**. It provides **no bot protection**; assume production replaces this visual challenge with non-interactive server-side abuse controls.

Before release, test keyboard-only operation, screen readers, mobile touch, 200% text resizing/400% zoom, expiry during review, payment failures and slow-network states.

